INT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE, DUSTY JUNCTION - DAY

Shuttered windows. Heat. A dull roar of hooves somewhere beyond the wall. A telegraph key clatters on its own.

MARSHAL ISADORA CRANE, 40s, grey duster, stands at the window, peering through a slat. NELL FENTON, 20s, the operator, taps the key with a pencil while OTTO BRAND, 60s, the stationmaster, stacks flour sacks against the door and LUPE ARRIAGA, 30s, a drover, holds a shotgun and checks the roof beams for cracks. Nobody speaks until the floor starts to shake.

CRANE
Nell. What does the line say?

NELL
It says "hold." Over and over.

OTTO
Hold what?

NELL
Hold fast, hold the line, hold your fire. It doesn't say.

LUPE
Who sent it?

NELL
Miles West. Eight miles up.

TOMMY (O.S.)
(faint, through the wall)
They're coming through! Don't let 'em split the herd!

CRANE
That's Tommy.

LUPE
Tommy's dead. We buried him Tuesday.

CRANE
Then someone sounds like him.

The shaking grows. Dust sifts from the ceiling. Otto stops stacking. Lupe lifts the shotgun. Nell grips the key. Crane lets the slat fall shut and draws her revolver, and the five of them stand there as the roar rises to a wall of sound.

CRANE (CONT'D)
Nobody fires till I do.

The roar passes. Slowly it fades.

Silence.

OTTO
Well.

NELL
It says "all clear."

CRANE
(dry)
That's a first.
